## Diff Editor: Toolbar Buttons

The toolbar sits at the top-right of the diff editor tab. These buttons control how the comparison is displayed and navigated. *- red rectangle* 

| Button | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| **Git: View File History** | Opens the Git history view for the current file, displaying a timeline of all commits that have modified it. From here, you can browse previous versions, compare older revisions, or see exactly when and by whom each change was introduced. |
| **Open File** | Opens the currently selected side (either the left or right version) in a standard editor tab outside the diff view. This gives you access to full editing features like IntelliSense, multi-line selection, and global search, while keeping the diff comparison open for reference. |
| **Open Preview to the side** | Opens a preview panel showing the **rendered output** of the assembled document. This lets you see how the changes in the underlying `.tsmap` or markdown source will appear in the final document, without leaving the diff view. |
| **Previous Change** | Jumps the cursor to the **previous block of changed lines** in the diff. Use this to step backwards through each modification. |
| **Next Change** | Jumps the cursor to the **next block of changed lines** in the diff. Use this to step forwards through each modification. |
| **Show leading/trailing whitespace differences** | Toggles the visibility of **whitespace characters** (spaces, tabs, and line endings) at the start and end of lines. In markdown and templating systems, whitespace can affect formatting and variable resolution, so this helps catch subtle changes that are otherwise invisible. |
| **Toggle collapse unchanged regions** | Collapses the long stretches of identical content between changes, leaving only the modified blocks visible. This reduces scrolling and keeps your focus on what actually changed. Click again to expand the full file. |
| **Split editor right** | Opens a new editor group to the **right** of the current diff, allowing you to view another file — such as a reference document or the live version — alongside the comparison. |

